{
  "question": "For the TCP wire protocol, which approach should we use? The assignment requires one general request/message-wrapping service reusable across nearly all client-callable methods. I recommend a shared `Request`/`Response` envelope using Java serialization: compact implementation, typed arguments/results, and no extra dependencies. A length-prefixed text protocol is more transparent but needs explicit parsing/escaping; JSON is readable but adds a library dependency.",
  "choices": [
    "Generic Java-serialized Request/Response envelopes (Recommended)",
    "Length-prefixed text protocol",
    "JSON messages"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "Yes—that’s the simple approach I recommend: send a serializable request object containing a command and its arguments, then receive a response object containing the result (or an error). A shared request factory/dispatcher can cover all operations without duplicating serialization code. Next decision: should the client open a fresh TCP connection for each command, send one request, receive one reply, and close it? That avoids framing and request-correlation machinery while matching the client’s blocking, one-command-at-a-time behavior.",
  "choices": [
    "One TCP connection per command (Recommended)",
    "Keep a persistent client connection"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "Got it: we’ll keep each client TCP connection open and exchange one request/response at a time. That means each connection’s handler can associate the response with its client without request IDs, while the middleware’s listener continues accepting other clients. For middleware-to-resource-manager calls, should we also open a fresh connection for each forwarded command, or keep reusable connections in a pool? I recommend one connection per forwarded request for simpler isolation and concurrency; the RM can still process many such connections concurrently.",
  "choices": [
    "One RM connection per request (Recommended)",
    "Persistent connection pool"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "Yes—keeping exactly three long-lived middleware-to-RM connections is reasonable and avoids repeated socket setup. TCP guarantees bytes are read in send order on each stream. The important distinction is that if an RM executes requests concurrently, their replies may finish out of order; we’ll need a small request ID in each envelope and a pending-request map so every reply reaches the right client. The middleware can keep one response-reader per RM stream, while the RM dispatches incoming requests to a worker pool. Shall we use that design, or do you prefer simpler strict FIFO execution per RM (which limits same-RM concurrency)?",
  "choices": [
    "Three persistent RM streams with request IDs and concurrent execution (Recommended)",
    "Three persistent RM streams with strict FIFO execution"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "Yes, non-persistent RM connections solve the *reply-correlation* problem simply: the middleware opens a socket for a request, sends it, reads that request’s reply on the same socket, then closes it. No request ID or shared response-reader is needed. But they do **not** guarantee operations execute in arrival order: a concurrent RM can still run a later request before an earlier one, so shared-state synchronization is still necessary. Tradeoff: simpler routing, but connection setup for every operation. Which do you prefer for the three middleware-to-RM paths?",
  "choices": [
    "One connection per request; simple reply routing (Recommended)",
    "Three persistent RM streams; request IDs for concurrent replies"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "Synchronization belongs in each RM’s request execution, not in TCP handling. The current ResourceManager synchronizes individual map reads/writes, but a reservation is a multi-step operation (read customer and inventory, update both), so two workers can interleave those steps and oversell inventory or lose a reservation. The simplest correct starting point is one lock per RM around each whole operation: `reserveFlight` locks while checking/decrementing inventory and updating the customer; add/delete/customer operations do likewise; queries take a read lock; and `bundle` holds the write lock for its full sequence/rollback. This still lets the middleware and RM handle multiple sockets/requests concurrently, allows concurrent queries, and lets the three separate RMs work in parallel, while serializing conflicting mutations within each RM. Per-item locks could allow more simultaneous writes but complicate operations touching both a customer and an item, plus bundles/deletes. Which synchronization approach should we choose?",
  "choices": [
    "One per-RM read/write lock; simple and safe (Recommended)",
    "Per-item/customer locks; more concurrent writes, more complex"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "Where should TCP customer records live? The least disruptive option is to keep a copy at each of the three RMs (as the existing RMI middleware does): the middleware fans out customer creation/deletion/bill queries, and each RM tracks that customer’s reservations for its own resource type. A dedicated customer service or middleware-owned customer store is more centralized but requires changing the current RM reservation logic and bill/deletion flows.",
  "choices": [
    "Replicate customer records at all three RMs (Recommended; matches existing design)",
    "Store customer records only in middleware",
    "Add a dedicated customer RM"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "If one part of a bundle fails after earlier reservations succeeded, what behavior should we implement? The current RMI middleware attempts rollback by deleting and recreating the customer, which risks losing unrelated reservations. A clean all-or-nothing bundle would require an internal cancellation operation on RMs (or a more elaborate prepare/commit protocol). Leaving successful reservations in place is simpler but produces a partial bundle.",
  "choices": [
    "All-or-nothing; rollback only the reservations made by this bundle (Recommended behavior, modest extra code)",
    "Partial success is acceptable; report bundle failure but keep earlier reservations"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "For middleware-to-RM traffic, the three persistent streams you suggested avoid repeated connection setup and preserve the order in which requests are written. But concurrent requests then need request IDs plus a response reader/pending map, and TCP order still does not guarantee execution order if RM workers run concurrently. With a fresh RM socket per request, each reply is naturally matched to its request and the implementation is simpler, but there is no shared FIFO across clients. Which tradeoff do you want?",
  "choices": [
    "One socket per request; simpler routing (Recommended)",
    "Three persistent streams; explicit request IDs"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "To keep the TCP version small and behaviorally consistent, should we reuse the existing console client unchanged and give it a TCP proxy that implements `IResourceManager`? Each proxy method can call one shared generic `sendRequest(command, args)` function, satisfying the assignment’s general message-wrapping requirement. The alternative is rewriting command execution directly around sockets, which duplicates substantial existing logic.",
  "choices": [
    "Reuse existing client with an IResourceManager TCP proxy (Recommended)",
    "Rewrite client commands specifically for TCP"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "How should server addresses and ports be configured? I recommend simple command-line overrides with localhost defaults: each RM takes an optional port; the middleware takes each RM’s host/port plus its own listen port; the client takes the middleware host/port. That supports local testing and the five-machine demo without editing Java constants. The alternative is hard-coded ports/hosts, simpler initially but inconvenient to deploy.",
  "choices": [
    "Command-line host/port options with localhost defaults (Recommended)",
    "Hard-code the demo hosts and ports"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "I recommend a fixed-size worker pool on both middleware and RMs (for example, 8 workers per process): it handles concurrent clients/requests but avoids creating unbounded threads. A thread-per-connection approach is simpler to read but can exhaust resources if clients keep connections open. Which should we use?",
  "choices": [
    "Fixed-size worker pools (Recommended)",
    "One thread per connection"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "Should bundle atomicity mean “if any step fails, undo only this bundle’s successful steps” (simpler compensating rollback; concurrent queries might briefly see intermediate reservations), or must no other request observe a partially completed bundle (requires broader cross-RM locking or prepare/commit coordination)?",
  "choices": [
    "Compensating rollback is enough (Recommended for this assignment)",
    "Require isolation from concurrent requests"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "Should we support optional port overrides for local testing while keeping 3042 as the default everywhere, or assume integration testing always uses separate machines and keep the scripts strictly at 3042?",
  "choices": [
    "Optional port overrides; default remains 3042 (Recommended)",
    "Only port 3042; test across separate machines"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "Agreed. I’d make the shared request envelope `Request implements Serializable` with `String command` and `String[] args` (or an empty array when there are no arguments); the TCP proxy can serialize typed method parameters into strings, and each RM handler dispatches by command. For the reply, should we use a simple `Response` object with `Object result` plus an optional error string? That covers the existing `boolean`, `int`, and `String` return types without separate response classes.",
  "choices": [
    "Yes: Object result plus optional error string",
    "Use a typed result field per response"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "Yes—we can avoid a generic `Response` class. The caller knows the method’s return type, so it can read a `boolean`, `int`, or `String` directly; `String` is a reference type, but it’s still one of the API’s simple return values. We do need to distinguish a transport/server error from a legitimate `false` result, though. Should each reply start with a small success/error status, followed by the typed value on success or an error message on failure?",
  "choices": [
    "Yes: status, then typed value/error message (Recommended)",
    "No status; let connection failures represent all errors"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "RMI automatically transports `RemoteException` objects, but raw TCP transports only bytes. The RM may throw `RemoteException`, and sockets can independently fail with I/O errors; the middleware needs to know whether it received a valid `false` result or an exception. We could close the socket and treat EOF as a generic failure, but that loses the RM’s error detail. I recommend an explicit error status plus message, and otherwise reading the result as its known type. Which behavior do you want?",
  "choices": [
    "Explicit error status and message; preserve failure detail (Recommended)",
    "Close on exception; treat EOF as a generic failure"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "Agreed. One clarification: the interface returns `boolean`, `int`, and `String` values, so the proxy knows which type to read based on the command. We can keep `Request` as just `String command` plus `String[] args` (all arguments converted to strings); each server parses numeric/boolean arguments at dispatch. Replies will be a success flag followed by the method’s typed result, or a failure flag plus error text. Ready for me to implement this protocol layer first, before the socket servers?",
  "choices": [
    "Implement the protocol layer first (Recommended)",
    "Continue discussing design before editing"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "Should the response also be a serializable envelope—`Object result` plus an optional error message—so the proxy casts/unboxes according to the method it called? That keeps the wire protocol uniform while distinguishing a legitimate `false`/`0`/empty-string result from an RM or socket failure.",
  "choices": [
    "Yes: generic Object result plus optional error (Recommended)",
    "No: status, then result written by its concrete type"
  ]
}

{
  "question": "Are the existing TCP deletions and edits intentional, and should I build the new protocol layer on top of the current working tree while preserving them?",
  "choices": [
    "Yes, intentional; proceed and preserve all existing changes",
    "No, I need to inspect or restore those changes first"
  ]
}

